The '''Emperor's Throne Room''' was a room reserved for [[Darth Sidious]], the [[Galactic Emperor]] of the [[Galactic Empire]] aboard the [[DS-2 Death Star II Mobile Battle Station]]. It was located at the pinnacle of the [[Emperor's Tower]] in the north pole of the [[battle station]]. The throne room was designed for the sole purpose of intimidating those who entered it. Four [[Standard year|years]] after the [[Battle of Yavin]], as a [[Battle of Endor|decisive encounter]] raged both in [[Realspace|space]] and on the surface of [[Endor]], the Emperor's Throne Room hosted the fateful duel between [[Anakin Skywalker|Darth Vader]] and his estranged son [[Luke Skywalker]].

==Description==
[[File:EmperorsTower-ROTJ.png|thumb|left|250px|250px|The Emperor's Throne Room was located in an isolation tower on the second Death Star.]]
Contrary to [[Galactic Emperor]] [[Darth Sidious|Sheev Palpatine's]] personal quarters and offices, both on the [[battle station]] and back in the [[Imperial Palace]] on [[Coruscant]], the [[DS-2 Death Star II Mobile Battle Station|second Death Star's]] throne room was devoid of ornaments and symbols of plush comfort. The bleak, industrial design was meant to frighten all those would enter the chamber, be it [[prisoner]]s, Imperial subjects, or even dignitaries.<ref name="Ultimate Star Wars">''[[Ultimate Star Wars]]''</ref> To approach the Emperor, visitors had to cross a narrow bridge that extended over a vast chasm. A mile below, the main [[reactor]] of the Death Star glowed an eerie blue [[color]]. Several sets of stairs had been placed in the room for no reason other than their own inconvenience.<ref name="beware" /> The [[throne]] itself was a simple, contoured swiveling chair set in front of a large circular [[viewport]] lined with a web-like framework and equipped with [[magnification scanner]]s. The dais before the Emperor was flanked by [[viewscreen]]s linked to the station's [[computer]]s and communications systems.<ref name="Ultimate Star Wars" /> There was a [[Death Star's holding room|holding room]], in which visitors seeking admittance in the throne room waited for the authorization.<ref name="beware" />

The room was not only Palpatine's ceremonial seat of power on the battle station, as it also served as the Emperor's command center,<ref name="Ultimate Star Wars" /> and Palpatine was able to send orders to his forces from the room.<ref name="The Levers of Power">{{StoryCite|story=The Levers of Power|book=The Rise of the Empire}}</ref> Located atop the high<ref name="CVD">''[[Star Wars: The Complete Visual Dictionary, New Edition]]''</ref> [[Emperor's Tower]]<ref name="Death Star Two Blueprints">{{SWCT|set=Blueprints|cardname=Death Star II}}</ref> on the second Death Star, the throne room gave Palpatine the chance to watch over the destruction<ref name="CVD" /> unleashed during the [[Battle of Endor]].<ref name="Episode VI" /> The room also featured some red lighting, as the Emperor favored using decor in that color. This also allowed for [[Emperor's Royal Guard|Imperial Royal Guards]] to blend into the room.<ref name="CVD" />

==History==
[[File:BountyHunters36-VirginVariant-Sprouse.png|thumb|right|200px|Darth Vader escorts his son into the Emperor's Throne Room on the Death Star.]]
{{Quote|All orders were coming from the Emperor himself, but the overbridge says there's no response from the throne room.''"<br />"''That's not possible. There's some mistake.|Ives and Emarr Ottkreg|<ref name="The Levers of Power" />}}
When the Emperor visited the Death Star shortly before his [[death]] during the Battle of [[Endor]], the throne room had only just been finished.<ref name="beware" /> The ultimate confrontation between [[Jedi]] and the [[Sith]] took place in the throne room when [[Luke Skywalker]], last heir of the Jedi ways, faced Palpatine and his [[Sith apprentice|apprentice]] [[Anakin Skywalker|Darth Vader]]. The Emperor taunted and tempted Skywalker, leading to [[Lightsaber duel|duel]] Vader, who was in fact the young Jedi's estranged [[Parent|father]]. Although Skywalker managed to cripple Vader, he refused to succumb to the [[Dark side of the Force|dark side]]. Enraged at the young man's denial, the Emperor unleashed deadly [[Force lightning]] into Skywalker. The Jedi's pleas for help awakened the good in Vader, who then chose to renounce his connection to the dark side and turned against the Emperor. While he had suffered fatal injuries from the Emperor's lightning, Vader managed to hurl him into the reactor shaft, which killed the tyrant, releasing a blaze of dark side energy.<ref name="Episode VI" />

[[File:A_Dyad_in_the_Force.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Rey and Kylo Ren confronted each other in the ruins of the throne room long after the second Death Star's destruction.]]
After the Death Star's destruction, the wreckage of the throne room crashed into the [[ocean]]s of the moon [[Kef Bir]]. Luke Skywalker's [[Padawan|Jedi apprentice]], the [[scavenger]] [[Rey Skywalker|Rey]], came to the wreck<ref name="Episode IX" /> in [[35 ABY]]<ref name="TROS Date">''[[Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker: The Visual Dictionary]]'' dates the events of ''Star Wars'': Episode IX ''The Rise of Skywalker'' as taking place one [[Standard year|year]] after the [[Hosnian Cataclysm|Starkiller Incident]], which ''[[Star Wars: Galactic Atlas]]'' dates to [[34 ABY]]. Therefore, the events of ''Star Wars'': Episode IX ''The Rise of Skywalker'' must take place in [[35 ABY]].</ref> to search for a [[Sith wayfinder]] that would lead her to the resurrected Sidious on [[Exegol]]. After she encountered a [[Force vision]] of herself fallen to the dark side, [[First Order]] [[Supreme Leader]] [[Ben Solo|Kylo Ren]], Sidious' new enforcer, appeared in the ruined throne room and destroyed the relic, and a [[Mission to Kef Bir|duel]] between the two ensued, which ultimately led to Ren renouncing the dark side of the Force.<ref name="Episode IX" />

==Behind the scenes==
===Conception===
